  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Windy start to the week with numerous rain showers \nacross the Northern Mountains of PA\n\nA fast-moving upper level trough and weak sfc cold front will \nbring occasional rain showers, mainly to the Northern Mountains\nof PA through the rest of this morning. A few of the showers\nwill sneak a layer or two of counties to the South of Interstate\n80. \n\nRainfall amounts will be just 0.01-0.02 of an inch near I-80, \nwith up to one-tenth of an inch in spots across the Northern \nMtns. no measurable rain is expected to the south of a line from\nAltoona to Lewistown, Selinsgrove and Pottsville, where \nsignificant downsloping and a period of strong subsidence \nbeneath the right exit, then left entrance regions of a 120 kt \nspeed max will occur. \n\nThe periods of deep subsidence and increased vertical mixing\nthroughout the boundary layer later this morning and early this\nafternoon will focus the strongest wind gusts - likely around 40\nmph from the NW.\n\nBreezy conditions will continue through the first part of the \nweek with temperatures rebounding modestly higher by Tuesday. No\nadditional precip is forecast through midweek. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: First accumulating snow of the season is likely \nfor late Thursday night through Friday Night - mainly across \nthe Northern and Western Mtns of PA\n\nA drastic turn to winter-like weather will occur late this week\nas a large and slow moving upper low becomes carved out across\nthe NE US and SE Canada by several rounds of digging jet energy\nand associated surges of progressively colder air.\n\nAs a result of the upper low becoming so expansive and several\nareas of low pressure at the sfc trying to consolidate into a\nsingle coastal low, a significant anchoring high to our NE\ncapable of pumping in colder llvl air and steep ascent\nassociated with favorable couple jet structure overhead looks \nto be lacking at this point.\n\nStill, ample deep layer moisture and 850 mb in the highly\nfavorable -2 to -5C range will lead to periods of rain\nThursday/Thursday evening transitioning to snow before midnight\nacross the NW Mtns and Laurel Highlands. More uncertainty in \nprecip type and amounts (after the daylight hours Thursday) \nexists across our Central Ridge and Valley zones depending on \nthe exact shape/and track of the mid-upper level low and \nindividual pieces of jet energy pivoting around its core(s). \n\n09/00Z EC and its ENS members are a more southern/colder \nsolution for more snow across our Central CWA.  \n\nCold air expanding southward behind an increasing NW wind could\ndrive lake effect and upslope precip Friday night into the \nweekend. Friday and Saturday are fcst to be the coldest days \nwith temperatures 5-15 degrees below normal.",
